sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch12-46> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "General features proposed as marks of intelligence (learning and memory, adaptation, abstraction) occur in systems such as simple cellular automata and fluids, so there is no workable general definition of intelligence; any usable one must be tied to specific details of human intelligence.";
    dc:type "conceptual";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "No general definition of intelligence independent of human details";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 12, “The Principle of Computational Equivalence”; section “Intelligence in the Universe” (from p. 822), p. 823. Scope: definitions of intelligence"
}
